Certainly! RAM (Random Access Memory) is a type of computer memory that allows data to be read or written in almost the same amount of time, regardless of the physical location of the data inside the memory. Here's an example in C++ code:
```
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
   int a = 10; // declare and initialize variable "a"
   int *p = &a; // declare pointer "p" and assign it the memory address of "a"

   cout << "Value of variable a: " << a << endl;
   cout << "Memory address of variable a: " << &a << endl;
   cout << "Value of pointer p: " << p << endl;
   cout << "Value stored at memory address pointed by p: " << *p << endl;
   *p = 20; // change the value stored at the memory address pointed by "p"
   cout << "New value of variable a: " << a << endl;
   return 0;
}
```
In this example, we declare a variable `a` and initialize it with the value of `10`. We also declare a pointer `p` and assign it the memory address of `a` using the `&` operator.
We then print out the value of `a`, the memory address of `a`, the value of `p`, and the value stored at the memory address pointed by `p` using the `*` operator.
Next, we change the value stored at the memory address pointed by `p` to `20` using the `*` operator again. This means that the value of `a` is also changed to `20`.
This example demonstrates how RAM works by allowing us to store and manipulate data in memory using pointers. The data is stored in memory locations that can be accessed quickly and efficiently, regardless of their physical location within the memory.

