How can I implement the concept of "cooperative competition" in a Go game? Can you provide an example of a program where multiple users compete while also working together towards a common goal?
For instance, could you demonstrate how to implement a game where players work together to achieve a high score, but also compete against each other for individual achievements? Please provide a code example that showcases this concept in action.
Here's an example implementation of a cooperative competition game in Go:
[Go code]
package main
import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)
var players = []string{"Alice", "Bob"}
var scores = make(map[string]int)
var mutex = sync.Mutex{}
func incrementScore(player string) {
	mutex.Lock()
	defer mutex.Unlock()
	scores[player]++
	updateScoreboard()
}
func updateScoreboard() {
	fmt.Println("Scoreboard:")
	for _, player := range players {
		score := scores[player]
		fmt.Printf("%s: %d\n", player, score)
	}
}
func main() {
	for _, player := range players {
		scores[player] = 0
	}
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	wg.Add(1)
	go func() {
		defer wg.Done()
		for {
			incrementScore("Alice")
		}
	}()
	wg.Add(1)
	go func() {
		defer wg.Done()
		for {
			incrementScore("Bob")
		}
	}()
	wg.Add(1)
	go func() {
		defer wg.Done()
		for {
			mutex.Lock()
			totalScore := scores["Alice"] + scores["Bob"]
			mutex.Unlock()
			if totalScore >= 10 {
				fmt.Println("Game over!")
				break
			}
		}
	}()
	wg.Wait()
}
In this game, Alice and Bob are both competing to get the highest score, but they can also work together by incrementing each other's scores. The game also has a global goal of reaching a total score of 10, at which point the game ends. The Go code includes a mutex to ensure that scores are incremented safely and a WaitGroup to wait for all goroutines to finish before ending the program.

