In a Go program, I have a list of grocery items with their prices. How can I select the two most expensive items from the list? Here is the list:
groceryList := []struct {
    item string
    price float64
}{
    {"eggs", 2.50},
    {"flour", 1.75},
    {"milk", 3.25},
    {"bananas", 0.75},
    {"cherries", 5.50},
    {"coffee", 7.99},
}
What Go code can I use to select the two most expensive items from this list based on their price?

output:
You can use the sort.Slice function to sort the groceryList slice by price in descending order, and then select the first two items from the sorted slice. Here is the Go code to do so:
```
import "sort"
func getTwoMostExpensiveItems(groceryList []struct{item string; price float64}) []string {
    // sort the groceryList slice by price in descending order
    sort.Slice(groceryList, func(i, j int) bool {
        return groceryList[i].price > groceryList[j].price
    })

    // select the first two items from the sorted slice
    var result []string
    for i := 0; i < 2; i++ {
        result = append(result, groceryList[i].item)
    }
    return result
}
// usage
groceryList := []struct {
    item string
    price float64
}{
    {"eggs", 2.50},
    {"flour", 1.75},
    {"milk", 3.25},
    {"bananas", 0.75},
    {"cherries", 5.50},
    {"coffee", 7.99},
}
mostExpensiveItems := getTwoMostExpensiveItems(groceryList)
fmt.Println(mostExpensiveItems) // prints ["coffee", "cherries"]
```

